From 282707b7fa8e8f323b9ebbe3abd51dd2daa24986 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:47:41 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 15/39] Metal diamond engine: split coefficient tables for
 graded meshes (snapshot cg27)

A graded PCB mesh has an operator for every few f4vectors (the SMA launch: 64410 operators
for 221490 f4vectors, 12.4 MB of packed coefficients next to 21 MB of fields). The split
layout keeps one table of the distinct coefficient f4vectors, vv and ii first (at most
1024, 10-bit indices), and per operator the indices of vi0 vi1 vi2 and vv0 | vv1 << 10 |
vv2 << 20 (ii, iv the same). The operators share most of their f4vectors (the SMA launch:
88485 of them, 1.4 MB, plus 2.1 MB of indices), so the caches hold the coefficients of
more cells, for one more dependent load per cell. Same float values in the same
operations.

Split is chosen when the packed coefficients of a 16x16-column block exceed 32 KB on
average (the setup line prints the figure); OPENEMS_DIAMOND_COEF=packed or split forces
one. Split against packed in this build, paired A/B: SMA launch (275 KB per block) x1.04
[1.02-1.07] and GCPW line (46 KB) x1.04 [1.03-1.06] at 4-5 other cores (an earlier
8-round session: x1.05 and x1.03, patch tutorial (58 KB) x1.00). On patch x5 (21 KB), the
notch tutorial (9 KB) and notch x3 (6 KB) the two layouts are within the noise (one
session with other GPU work had split 7 % behind on notch x3), so those keep packed.

Against cg24 (paired, 5 rounds, 4-5 other cores): SMA launch x1.27 [1.22-1.28], GCPW line
x1.30 [1.29-1.37], notch x3 x1.01, patch x5 x0.99, notch tutorial x0.98 [0.93-1.09].

emsKit diamond_check.sh 54 of 54, and 54 of 54 with OPENEMS_DIAMOND_COEF=split. All six
benchmark models bit-identical to the multithreaded engine with OPENEMS_FTZ=1.

